  "rationale": "Prematurity at 30 weeks with onset of respiratory distress within a few hours of birth is classic for neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (hyaline membrane disease).",
  "optionExplanations": {
    "A": "Usually occurs in term or post-term infants after meconium passage, not a 30-week premature neonate.",
    "B": "Correct: prematurity, early onset respiratory distress, and grunting strongly suggest surfactant deficiency causing neonatal RDS.",
    "C": "Can cause neonatal respiratory distress, but the gestational age and timing are more typical of RDS.",
    "D": "May present with sepsis and respiratory symptoms, but is less specifically associated with prematurity and early grunting than RDS.",
    "E": "Typically occurs in term or late preterm infants and is more common after elective caesarean delivery; less likely at 30 weeks."
  },
